" :answer-field/choices ["q-339879/field-5/0bf1508834bc49a787d08b8b33b37a3b65ce7dcf0b50406376ecc50165a704b6" "q-339879/field-5/f073cec77042b855fcc95ae59e5c07a0d566930857e27de8effde7dcda1f144f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339879" :question/id #uuid "703a7a55-d89b-5466-a847-c7db23ef2a30" :question/math-academy-id "q-339879" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y=-12{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{-2x})^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(-2{e}^{-2x})^{′}=4{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(4{e}^{-2x})+5(-2{e}^{-2x})-6({e}^{-2x})=-12{e}^{-2x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{-2x})^{′}={e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x})^{′}=-4{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(-4{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x})+5({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x})-6(x{e}^{-2x})=-12{e}^{-2x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}+{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339879/field-1" "q-339879/field-2" "q-339879/field-3" "q-339879/field-4" "q-339879/field-5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339879"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
